%\newcommand{\test}{$\sqrt{s_{NN}}$\xspace}
Full jet reconstruction in a heavy ion environment and while it has recently been demonstrated that it is possible\cite{jetrecoSevilWWND,jetrecoJoernHP,jetrecoSevilHP,jetrecoSevilHQ}, initial studies of jets were done by looking at correlations between high-\pT hadrons.  These studies remain valuable even now that full jet reconstruction has been done in heavy ion collisions because the biases are different in the two different methods.  Full jet reconstruction may be more biased towards unmodified jets because jet finding algorithms are dependent on jet topologies similar to those in \ee and \pp collisions.

In di-hadron correlations, a high momentum trigger particle is selected and the distribution of particles relative to that particle is determined.  No requirement other than the momentum is used to determine trigger particles and the method neglects contributions from any sources of high-\pT particles other than jets.  The momenta of the trigger and associated particles is restricted to increase the probability of the trigger particle coming from a jet and decrease the combinatorial background.  It has been argued that contributions from other sources are non-negligible and that such considerations are necessary for proper interpretation of the results of di-hadron correlations[add citations, phenix paper].  STAR has presented correlations normalized per trigger particle and with this normalization, results from different systems (\pp,\dAu, \Cu, and \Au) would be identical if there were no modification of the jet[cite previous STAR studies].  PHENIX has typically used a different normalization where the amplitude of the correlation is interpreted as the probability of a low momentum particle being correlated with the jet [PHENIX zyam paper and PHENIX correlation papers].  For the studies presented here, the correlation is normalized per trigger.



To determine the di-hadron correlation, the good events must be selected and, in most cases, correlations are performed in separate centrality bins in order to study effects which may be dependent on the size of the medium.  If identified particles are used for correlation studies, these particles must be identified with sufficient purity that contributions from other particles are negligible.  The distribution of particles relative to the trigger particle, averaged over all events, is calculated and this must be corrected for the efficiency for reconstructing the associated particles in the STAR detector.  A combinatorial background, which includes known correlations between particles which are assumed to be unrelated to jets, must be subtracted.  Different features in the correlations are then subtracted out 

\section{Event selection}
Data from \Au collisions at \sNNsixtytwo from are from RHIC's fourth year run (2004) and data from \Cu collisions at \sNNsixtytwo and \sNNtwohundred are from RHIC's sixth year run (2005.)  These data are compared to previous studies done in the \Au data at \sNNsixtytwo.  In order to be usable for analyses, events must have a clearly defined vertex.  Events from \Au data have a more clearly defined vertex because there are more tracks produced in a \Au collision which can be used to reconstruct the vertex position, so the 80\% most central events were usable in \Au collisions.  Fewer tracks are produced in \Cu collisions and the luminosity was significantly higher in 2005 than in 2006, so only the 60\% most central data from these events was usable.
