\section{Particle identification}
While the STAR TPC is only sensitive to charged hadrons, particles which decay into charged hadrons before they leave the TPC can identified by reconstructing their decay vertices.  The \lam, \alam, and \kaon identified by reconstructing their decay vertices are called \vzero's because they are neutral decay vertices.  Particles which decay into a \lam or \alam can also be reconstructed by first reconstructing the tertiary decay vertex of the \lam or \alam and then reconstructing the secondary decay vertex.  This method has been successfully applied by STAR to reconstruct the \xibary and \omegabary and their respective anti-particles through their decay channels \xidecay and \omegadecay.  Data on these particles and their decay channels is given in \Tref{TableV0Decays}.  This method has been used extensively by STAR to study the \lam, \alam, \xibary, \axibary, \omegabary, and \aomegabary baryons and the \kaon meson through their hadronic decay channels  \cite{STARForwardLambdas,STARStrangenessEnhancement,STARAuAuV0Flow,STARKaonHBT,STARStrangePP200,STARHyperonScaling,STARProtonLambdaCorrelations,STARMultistrangeFlowAuAu200,STARMultistrangeBaryonAuAu130,STARParticleDependentV2AuAu200,STARKaonProductionAuAu130,STARV0AuAu130,STARLambdaAuAu130}.

Correlations of \vzeros, \xibary, and \omegabary are only presented for \Cu at \sNNtwohundred.  The data from \Cu and \Au collisions at \sNNsixtytwo were investigated but the statistics did not allow for meaningful studies, and previous studies were done in \Au  at \sNNtwohundred \cite{JanaFrance,JanaWWND2007,JanaQM06,JanaHP06,JanaPANIC05}.

