\subsection{\vzero reconstruction}

\begin{figure}
  \centering\resizebox{9cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/v0geometriccuts.eps}}\\

  \caption[Schematic diagram of \vzero decay.]
  {Schematic diagram of a \lam decay showing the different parameters that can be varied when geometrically reconstructing a \vzero from its decay vertex.
  \label{V0Schematic}}
\end{figure}

A schematic diagram of the geometry of a \lam decay is shown in \Fref{V0Schematic}.  The \kaon decay is similar except for the identity of the daughters.  This shows the different parameters which can be varied to alter the purity and the efficiency of \vzero's.

The decay daughters of a \vzero should not originate from the primary vertex, however, because the distribution of decay lengths in a sample of \vzeros is falls off exponentially, a larger proportion of \vzeros will have decay daughters which are closer to the primary vertex than the detector resolution.  To get a sample of \vzero candidates, all pairs of global tracks, those which were detected but not forced to go through the primary vertex, are combined.  The mass is reconstructed assuming that they are the decay daughters of a \vzero.  The mass of a \vzero candidate is given by 

$m_{\Lambda} = (m_{\pi}+m_{p})^2 - (p_{x,\pi^-}+p_{x,p})^2- (p_{y,\pi^-}+p_{y,p})^2- (p_{z,\pi^-}+p_{z,p})^2$

$m_{\bar{\Lambda}} = (m_{\pi}+m_{p})^2 - (p_{x,\pi^+}+p_{x,\bar{p}})^2- (p_{y,\pi^+}+p_{y,\bar{p}})^2- (p_{z,
\pi^+}+p_{z,\bar{p}})^2$

$m_{K^0_S} = (m_{\pi}+m_{\pi})^2 - (p_{x,\pi^+}+p_{x,\pi^-})^2- (p_{y,\pi^+}+p_{y,\pi^-})^2- (p_{z,\pi^+}+p_{z,\pi^-})^2$,

with the momentum given by 

$\overrightarrow{p_{\Lambda}} = \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^-}} + \overrightarrow{p_{p}}$;
$\overrightarrow{p_{\bar{\Lambda}}} = \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^+}} + \overrightarrow{p_{\bar{p}}}$;
$\overrightarrow{p_{K^0_S}} = \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^-}} + \overrightarrow{p_{\pi^+}}$.
The identity of the daughter tracks is assigned randomly so the restrictions on the geometry of the decay must be sufficient to allow clean identification of tracks.  The combinatorial background in a heavy ion collision is considerably higher than in \ee or \pp collisions, and is larger at higher particle multiplicities.  This means that the background is greater in central collisions than in peripheral collisions.  The background is dominated by primary tracks, or those which originate from the primary vertex (PV.)  \vzero candidates are found when STAR reconstructs the events from the hits in the TPC, and to keep file sizes reasonable some geometric cuts are applied at production.  In addition, some cuts on track quality and particle identification were applied.  The list of default cuts is given in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts}.

\begin{table}
\begin{center}
\caption[Default geometric cuts for \vzeros]{Default geometric cuts for \vzero candidates.}\label{TableV0DefaultCuts}
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c}
\hline
  	 & $p_{T}^{V^0}<3.5 $ & $p_{T}^{V^0}<3.5 $ \\ \hline
  DCA of \vzero to PV &  $<$ 0.8 cm & $<$ 0.8 cm \\
  DCA of daughters &   $<$ 0.8  cm & $<$ 0.8  cm \\ 
  DCA of daughters to PV & $>$ 0.7 cm & $>$ 0 cm \\
  Decay length &  $>$ 2 cm &  $>$ 2 cm \\
  \nhits on daughter track & $>$ 15 &  $>$ 15 \\ 
  \nsigdedx & -3 $<$ \nsigdedx $<$ 3 & -3 $<$ \nsigdedx $<$ 3 \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\end{table}

