  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/geoCuts/V0s/Plots/AntiLambdaDcaDaughtersPt10_15_cent1.eps}
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/geoCuts/V0s/Plots/K0DcaDaughtersPt10_15_cent1.eps}
  }\\
  \centering\resizebox{14cm}{!}{
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/geoCuts/V0s/Plots/LambdaDcaV0ToPVPt10_15_cent1.eps}
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/geoCuts/V0s/Plots/AntiLambdaDcaV0ToPVPt10_15_cent1.eps}
  \includegraphics{/home/madscientistgirl/HarrisLab/Thesis/latex/analysis/geoCuts/V0s/Plots/K0DcaV0ToPVPt10_15_cent1.eps}
  }\\

  \caption[DCA of \vzero to primary vertex and of \vzero daughters to each other]
  {DCA of \vzero to primary vertex and of \vzero daughters to each other for \vzero candidates between 1.0 \GeV $<$ \pT $<$ 1.5 \GeV versus the reconstructed mass of the candidate.  These were produced with the default cuts in \Tref{TableV0DefaultCuts}.
  \label{DCADaughtersV0s}}
\end{figure}

\Fref{DCADaughtersV0s} shows the DCA of the daughter tracks to each other and two the primary vertex versus the mass of the \vzero candidate.  Plots such as these, with the parameter on the y-axis and the mass of the candidate on the x-axis, easily show what values of the parameter the background has.  These plots show similar patterns for both the DCA of the daughters and the DCA of the \vzero to the primary vertex because these paramters are dominated by the detector resolution.  Restrictions on the DCA of the \vzero daughters to each other and to the primary vertex eliminate background efficiently, and future detector upgrades at STAR will allow tighter restrictions on these paramters.  A single hit in the STAR TPC has a resolution of about 1 cm.  A track reconstructed from more hits will have a slightly better resolution, but this resolution is dependent on the momentum of the daughter tracks and the number of hits from the TPC used to form the track.  Lower momentum daughter tracks are not only more likely to have fewer hits in the TPC, but are have lost a greater fraction of their energy before they enter the TPC.  This means the resolution on the DCA of the daughter tracks will be worse because the location of the track near the secondary vertex must be further extrapolated than for high momentum daughter tracks.  \Fref{DCAK0ToPVvsPt} shows the reconstructed DCA in different bins in \pT.  The fact that the resolution gets better with higher momentum demonstrates that this method does better, not worse, at higher \pT and that currently studies using secondary vertex reconstruction in STAR are limited by statistics rather than the method.

