  \label{DCADaughtToPVDecay}}
\end{figure}

\Fref{DCADaughtToPVDecay} shows the the distribution of the decay daughters' DCA to the primary vertex and of the decay length versus the \pT of the \vzero candidate.  If there were no background, \vzeros could be reconstructed without applying any restrictions to the DCA of the decay daughters to the primary vertex and on the decay length.  However, since most of the background comes from primary tracks, most of the background has a small decay length and the daughter tracks are close to the primary vertex.  Since the resolution of a hit in the TPC is around 1 cm, most of the background tracks have daughter tracks about 1 cm or less from the primary vertex and a decay length of a few centimeters.  Since the background is also where most of the signal is, these cuts are inefficient, however, they also are very effective at reducing the background.  An increase in the detector resolution would only be effective to the extent that it would reduce the average distance from the primary vertex of a primary track and thus allow less signal to be eliminated by restricting the DCA of the daughter tracks.

Limits on the number of hits in the daughter tracks are also applied as quality cuts on the track reconstructed in the TPC.  Tracks with too few hits have poor momentum resolution and may originate from split tracks or from random background.  A minimum of 15 hits was required for all daughter tracks.  Below this most tracks have poor momentum resolution and may originate from split tracks.


